    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-33316",
      "knowledge_point_id": "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line",
      "problem": "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(-2,2,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-4t \\\\ y=1+6t \\\\ z=-1+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-2,2,0⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-4t \\\\ y=1+6t \\\\ z=-1+2t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨3,1,-1⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-4,6,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-4,6,2⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-4,6,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =⟨-2,2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩=⟨-2,2,0⟩"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩=20"
            },
